all vertices adjacent to `c`. Observing the set of vertices with function value 0, they would be neighbors of c if there's only one vertex with function value 2.\\n\\n**Deduction on ballWithin size**\\n\\nIf there is only one vertex `c` where `f c = 2`, then the set of vertices with `f(u) = 0` are all neighbors of `c`, implying this set is a subset of the ball of radius 1 around `c`. Also, `c` itself is within the ball.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing the ballWithin size**\\n\\nThe size of the set $\\\\{c\\\\} \\\\cup V_0$ can be calculated as $n - |V_1|$, where `n` is the total number of vertices and $|V_1| = \\\\text{romanWeight } f - 2$. The difference between `n` and the size of $\\\\{c\\\\} \\\\cup V_0$ is at most $\\\\text{romanWeight } f - 2$.\\n\\n**Deducing the maximum steps**\\n\\nBy the `card_ballWithin_succ_of_ne_univ` rule, at each step at least one vertex is added. After at most $\\\\text{romanWeight } f - 2$ further steps, the ball reaches the complete vertex set (`univ`). Therefore, the eccentricity of `c` is at most $\\\\text{romanWeight } f - 1$, and consequently, the graph \\n<truncated 8356 bytes>\\n leverages the fact that vertices with zero value are dominated by vertices of value 2. An average bound can then be computed.\\n\\n**Bounding eccentricity directly**\\n\\nThe derived average bound can then be used to construct a bound on the eccentricity of at least one vertex with the assumption that Roman weight is at most the graph's cardinality minus the vertices of function value 1. Then we need to ensure that the eccentricity is not too large.\\n\\n**Reconsidering the `P6` Graph**\\n\\nRe-examining the counterexample P6 to check for errors in the previous assumptions. The eccentricity of the graph's center in this case must be considered in relation to a bound. The center of the P6 graph is NOT vertices with value 2.\\n\\n**Revisiting Lemma Relevance**\\n\\nRecalling the provided lemmas: `(nontrivial) ⇒ (radius + 1) ≤ roman_domination_number` and the lemma